## 🛫 PILOT — Ticket Pipeline

Feature and technical work moves through **PILOT**, a lightweight multi-agent ticket
pipeline: specialized agent personas take a ticket from a raw idea to a merged PR, at a
fraction of the usual token cost, because each phase runs in its own short, focused
context instead of one big context carrying the whole history. PILOT's own
skills/agents/docs are copied in from
[`Jonas-Eve/pilot`](https://github.com/Jonas-Eve/pilot), not written here from scratch —
run `/pilot-update` to pull in that repo's process-doc/label/skill updates.

Six phases, each with its own Claude Code skill and agent persona:

| Phase | Skill | Agent | Produces |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Plan | `/pilot-story` | PM, or Architect for a technical need or bug report (auto-detected; `--tech`/`--bug` to declare upfront) | A functional user story, a formalized technical need, or a bug report |
| 2. Investigate | `/pilot-scope` | Architect (+ PM when a feature story is split) | Scoped ticket (tech/bug) or tasks with security/architecture decisions — mandatory for a feature story: dev task(s) plus one end-to-end-test task |
| 3. Lay out | `/pilot-spec` | Tech Lead | A technical implementation spec (or a test plan, for the e2e task) |
| 4. Operate | `/pilot-dev` | Senior Dev, or the e2e persona for the end-to-end-test task | Code + an open pull request |
| 5. Test & validate | `/pilot-review` | PM + Architect + Tech Lead (feature) or Architect + Tech Lead (tech/bug) | A consolidated go/no-go GitHub comment |
| 6. Human QA (feature stories only) | `/pilot-qa` | QA persona, paired with a human | A human-confirmed done, once every task has merged |

Every ticket goes through phase 1 first, whether it's a functional feature, a purely
technical need (CI, security, deployment, migrations, optimization), or a bug report —
`/pilot-story` auto-detects which agent should draft it (or `--tech`/`--bug` declares it
upfront), so there's never a separate ticket-creation path to remember. There's no single
command that runs all six phases end to end — each phase is driven on its own, one at a
time — and each also runs bare, with no argument at all, which is what lets a scheduled
cron Routine drive a phase on a timer instead of a human typing it each time.
`/pilot-story` and `/pilot-qa` always run paired with a human (no Routine for either,
though `--resume` picks a paused session back up); `/pilot-scope`, `/pilot-spec`, and
`/pilot-dev` default to that same pair mode too, so their Routine's prompt adds `--auto`
to run unattended; `/pilot-review` is already automatic and needs no flag.
A GitHub label state machine (`status:draft` → `status:backlog` → ... → `in-review` →
`approved` → `done`, plus `priority:`, `type:feature`/`type:tech`/`type:bug`, and a
`level:epic`/`status:split`
tracking hierarchy for work too big for one story) tracks where a ticket is and stops
multiple agents from picking up the same one — including a `status:changes-requested`
loop back to the dev phase when review finds something that needs an actual code fix, as
opposed to a pure judgment call. A `type:feature` story never reaches `done` straight from
its tasks, either: it passes through `status:qa` → `status:in-qa` first, phase 6's
human confirmation, before `/pilot-qa` itself sets `done`. Two flags sit orthogonal to
that state machine, each
cleared by a human once resolved: `needs-human` is what a phase adds when it hits
something only a person can decide right now; `on-hold` instead pauses a ticket for
something bigger in flight elsewhere (a restructuring, a dependency on other work) with
no immediate decision needed. Agents can propose a merge is ready, but a human always
performs the actual merge.

Each phase is a Claude Code slash command (`/pilot-story "..."`, `/pilot-scope 42`,
`/pilot-dev 42`, ...) — see [docs/pilot-process.md](docs/pilot-process.md) §0 for the
required GitHub labels (created once, not auto-created by any skill) and a full command
quickstart, and the rest of that doc for the state machine and claim protocol in detail.
